{"input_text": "Question: Let  $n > 1$  be an odd number. Let  $A$  be an  $n \times n$  matrix defined as follows
 \begin{equation}
\label{wams} a_{i, j} = \begin{cases} 1, & \text{for}\ i - j  \equiv \pm 2 \pmod n\\ 2, & \text{for}\ i = j\\ 0, & \text{otherwise}.\end{cases}
\end{equation} 
Calculate the determinant of matrix  $A$ . 

 Could someone please give me a hint for this question? I am completely green. I have tried at my best level, and still am not able to come up with a solution. 

Answer: Inverse element  $x^{-1}$  is homogenous. 
 In fact we take homogenous decomposition  $x^{-1}=y_1+\cdots+y_k$  where  $y_i$  is homogenous element s.t.  $\deg(y_i)\neq \deg (y_j)$ . 
 Then  $xy_1+\cdots+xy_k=xx^{-1}=1\in A_e$ , but because of  $\deg(xy_i)\neq \deg(xy_j)$  we get  $k=1$ . It means  $x^{-1}$  is homogenous. 
